        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: दन्तः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "दन्तः (dantaḥ) — \"tooth\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "दन्तः (dantaḥ) is a tooth, and its family is as secure as पादः (pādaḥ). Latin dens, in its other forms dentem — whence dentist, dental, trident, the three-tooth. Greek odous, odontos — whence mastodon."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English tooth belongs here too, by the same Germanic shift that turned p into f: an old d becomes t. So दन्तः (dantaḥ), dens and tooth are three worn copies of one word, and you have now seen the same machinery produce पिता/father, पादः (pādaḥ)/foot and दन्तः (dantaḥ)/tooth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A third demonstration of one sound law, on a word you can point at."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
